helper: slower rounds while the screen is off on battery ([poller] poll_s_display_off)

Out-of-tree patch for the y700 (TB323FU) running tb323fu-linux helper 0.4.2
(tag helper-v0.4.2 = 43ee432, the dpkg package tb323fu-helper 0.4.2 on the
tablet). Not submitted upstream. See y700/linux/power/WAKEUPS.md section 2.2.

History: the first version (2026-10-16, against 0.4.1) slept a flat 30 s
whenever the DSI panel was DPMS Off. It was never built or installed. 0.4.2
added charge_tick (the "current" Bypass method, the stall rescue, and the
Raise/Low/Wait stages of a charging restart), which steps once per 5 s
round, so a flat slow round would stretch those stages while charging with
the screen off. This version (2026-11-08) is rewritten for 0.4.2: the rounds
slow down only when nothing time-staged can run.

Why: with the screen off the tablet idles for hours without suspending
(README "永不休眠"). tb323fu-helperd still wakes a CPU every 5 s from two
threads: the poll loop in main.rs (profile/thermal/panel/haptics/full-by/
charge ticks, then PropertiesChanged snapshots of every D-Bus object, which
read the battery over glink) and the LED-ring thread (it re-reads the charge
color every 5 s, another battery read). Every battery attribute is a glink
round trip to the ADSP. Measured 2026-11-03 with the screen off: 9.62 context
switches/s for the daemon (10-16, same method: 9.9/s). The runtime
CX-collapse work (cx-collapse/RESEARCH.md 1.4) needs the system-wide wakeup
rate under 50/s, so every periodic poller counts.

What:
- config.rs: a new [poller] section, `poll_s_display_off = 30`. serde default:
  a helper.toml without it loads as before; 0 (or anything not longer than
  5 s) keeps the 5 s round. `Poller::round()` picks the wait; unit test
  `poller_round`.
- main.rs, end of each round: quiet = the screen is off (`perf::screen_off()`,
  upstream's own test: backlight bl_power != 0 or actual_brightness == 0)
  AND no external power (`f::external_power()`) AND no charging restart is
  armed (`rt.arm`) AND the thermal target is not "performance" (its heat
  guard ends performance from thermal_tick). Quiet: the next round comes
  poll_s_display_off later, but every 5 s a look (the backlight in sysfs,
  power_supply online: about two glink reads, so the main thread still
  wakes every 5 s) ends the wait at once when the screen comes on or a
  charger appears. Not quiet: 5 s, as before.
- led.rs: the main loop tells the ring its pace (`Led::set_quiet`). While
  quiet the charge color is re-read every poll_s_display_off. A change of
  pace (a charger came or went, the screen came on) re-reads it at once
  without restarting a hardware breathing pattern (`kick` would), and once
  more 5 s later (the battery status can lag the charger by a moment). The
  flag lives under the LED's own lock, so the LED thread never takes `rt`.
- data/helper.toml.example: the key.

Side effects, only while quiet (screen off, on battery):
- PropertiesChanged of every object (battery percentage, ...) comes up to
  poll_s_display_off late. Nobody looks with the screen off; D-Bus method
  calls (tb323fu-ctl, the GNOME extension, our control centre) still answer
  live.
- Charger plugged in: seen within 5 s, then a full round runs at once (the
  charge tick, PropertiesChanged) and the pace goes back to 5 s. The LED
  shows the charging color within about 5-10 s (stock: within 5 s).
- Unplugged: the ring re-reads at once and 5 s later, so it does not stay
  amber for a whole slow round.
- wifi_tick re-checks performance-profile Wi-Fi power saving every 12 rounds
  (1 min); while quiet that is about 6 min. Only matters with
  [wifi] low_latency_performance and the performance GPU profile.
- haptics_tick (a reloaded motor driver) and the USB wake re-assert come
  every 30 s instead of 5 s. The kernel auto-check is time based.
- A power-profiles-daemon profile change applies up to 30 s late. A
  charging restart armed over D-Bus (set_bypass, Reload) waits up to 30 s
  for its first step; harmless on battery, and a charger ends the wait.
  Setting thermal "performance" over D-Bus runs thermal_tick at once and
  turns the 5 s rounds back on from the next round.
- A Reload that changes poll_s_display_off applies from the next round (the
  LED ring at once, through kick).
- Screen on, or on a charger: nothing changes.

Not done on purpose:
- The 5 s look could read only the UCSI supplies (ucsi-source-psy*/online,
  answered from the kernel's cached connector status, no glink), about 15 %
  fewer glink requests per 30 s. Not done: a second charger test next to
  upstream's, and nobody checked on the tablet that UCSI reports a plain
  5 V (non-PD) charger. A missed report would cost one slow round.
- No tb323fu-ctl sub-command (edit helper.toml, `tb323fu-ctl reload`), no
  docs/helper-reference.md entry, not sent upstream.

Expected (estimate, not measured): about 9.6 -> 2.5 context switches/s for
the daemon with the screen off on battery: glink reads per 30 s about four fifths fewer
(most of the context switches), timer wakeups per 30 s 12 -> 7 (the 5 s
look stays).

Build on the tablet (no cargo on the Win11 side). Rust >= 1.82 in practice
(upstream already uses Option::is_none_or; helper/README.md says 1.80).
The tablet's ~/.cargo registry lacks about 28 crates the Linux target needs
(minisign-verify and others): one `cargo fetch` from crates.io, about 2-3 MB.
That download needs the owner's OK (2026-11-08: "先不下", not yet).
    # on Win11, in the y700 repo:
    git -C linux/tb323fu-linux archive --prefix=helper-0.4.2/ -o "$TEMP/helper-0.4.2.tar" helper-v0.4.2 helper
    scp -O "$TEMP/helper-0.4.2.tar" linux/power/helperd-slow-poll.patch root@<tablet>:/usr/local/src/
    # on the tablet, as root:
    cd /usr/local/src && tar xf helper-0.4.2.tar && cd helper-0.4.2
    patch -p1 --dry-run < ../helperd-slow-poll.patch && patch -p1 < ../helperd-slow-poll.patch
    cd helper
    cargo fetch --locked --target aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu     # the download, owner's OK first
    cargo test --offline --locked -p tb323fu-helper-core
    cargo build --release --offline --locked -p tb323fu-helperd
    install -m755 target/release/tb323fu-helperd /usr/local/libexec/tb323fu-helperd
    # dpkg owns /usr/libexec/tb323fu/tb323fu-helperd and the unit: never replace
    # them, point the unit at our binary with a drop-in (survives upgrades).
    # The helper's self-update still refuses: it looks at the dpkg-owned polkit
    # policy and D-Bus service files too (checked on the tablet 2026-11-08).
    mkdir -p /etc/systemd/system/tb323fu-helperd.service.d
    printf '[Service]\nExecStart=\nExecStart=/usr/local/libexec/tb323fu-helperd\n' \
        > /etc/systemd/system/tb323fu-helperd.service.d/y700-local.conf
    systemctl daemon-reload && systemctl restart tb323fu-helperd   # say so first: the LED ring and
                                                                    # D-Bus answers blink for a second
    tb323fu-ctl status
Verify: y700-checklist prints OK "tb323fu-helperd 跑的是自编的熄屏慢轮询版";
y700-wakeups 60 --off (screen off, on battery, no ssh during the run) shows
tb323fu-helperd far below 9.6/s; plug a charger with the screen off and the
ring turns amber within about 10 s.
Undo: rm the drop-in, systemctl daemon-reload, systemctl restart
tb323fu-helperd. The stock daemon ignores the [poller] key (no
deny_unknown_fields) and drops it on its next save.

Rebuild after every tb323fu-helper package upgrade (y700-checklist warns when
the running binary is not ours, and reports CHANGED when the package version
changes): re-apply this patch on the new tag first; it touches the same three
waits that 0.4.7/0.4.8 changed in led.rs, so expect to rework it.

